You can, indeed connect the serial ports to control the Alpha machine from 
your windows machine.  There are a few details here though:

I'm not familiar with the term "nullmodemcable," instead, I use a serial-
cross-over cable.  The terminal settings (unless you specifically changed 
them) are 9600-8-n-1.

Not all OSes handle a console-on-serial very well during install.  The best 
(of course) is Tru64, because it's the only one made exclusively for 
Alphas.  (Maybe I'm eating my words by not mentioning VMS.)  I don't know 
about NetBSD.  
For SuSE, here's what I can say:
6.4 -- >>> b dka4 -fl 1
    	If your CD is dka4, boot the cd with this flag, and 
    	console goes to serial. When you reboot, you will again have 
    	console on serial.
7.0 -- Same as above, except:
    	When you reboot, you won't have a console on serial.
    	You need to boot a rescue disk (I suggest JumpStart)
    	and modify the startup files to put a console on serial, and
    	allow root login.


