Oh, about the specific error message you got:
It's a kind of generic one.
No init found simply means it couldn't find init; there are a thousand 
reasons why.  In your case, it couldn't find anything to mount as root.  It 
could be that there's no device, device not ready, no driver, mis-typed 
boot option, bad disk, i/o error on the boot disk, faulty disk image, bad 
sector, improper scsi emulation or lack thereof, etc etc...

Because it can't find init, the kernel's default error message is "try 
telling me where to find init with init="  But that's not your real 
problem.  The default init is indeed the correct init.  I think it's 
/sbin/init, but whatever.  In nearly all cases, you can solve this problem 
by figuring out the proper boot technique.  i.e. Insert a good disk, insert 
the right good disk, boot the right disk, boot the right kernel, with the 
right boot options, and have no other problems that impede booting.  
That is why so many of us are offering suggestions about things that might 
be wrong, an ignore the actual error message.

I don't honestly know what "unable to open initial console" means.


