regular CD in your cd-rw drive, through the regular hda or hdc or whatever 
interface.

Because the howto tells you to pass boot options to the kernel by way of 
lilo, and because you're using SRM/aboot instead, this might help some:

If you usually use the command 
    	>>> b dka0 -fi vmlinuz -fl "root=/dev/sda1"
And the howto says you need to pass the option "hdc=ide-scsi" then you 
should change your boot command to
    	>>> b dka0 -fi vmlinuz -fl "root=/dev/sda1 hdc=ide-scsi"

If you usually use a command like
    	>>> b dka0 -fl 0
Then you need to look in your /etc/aboot.conf file.  You will see the first 
line that starts with a 0, has the name of a kernel (Usually something like 
1/boot/vmlinuz) and some boot options.
Rather than changing your aboot.conf file right away, I suggest this:  
Write down what it says there, and figure out how to boot using the long 
command instead.  The aboot.conf file is just there to make your boot 
command shorter, and you don't want to mess it up until you know you can 
fix your problem that way.

How to tell if it's working:
(Do you have to login as root?)
type the command 
    	cdrecord -scanbus
If you get a list of scsi devices, and your cd-burner is listed among them, 
then it's working.
Also, you can try the command:
    	insmod ide-scsi
I know that this is necessary on my K6-2 computer with ide-cdrw drive, so 
it *might* work for you.


