The XL series was a system that could be either Intel-based or
Alpha-based.  The Intel variant was known as the DEC Celebris XL,
whereas the Alpha variant was known as the DEC Alpha XL.  The number
indicated the speed of the CPU (300 MHz in your case).  My company
purchased quite a few of the Intel versions.  To switch between
versions, you'd just purchase a new  daughter riser card with the
Alpha or Intel processor on it and swap it out with the old one.

The Systems Option Catalog for the Alpha variant is located here:
http://www.compaq.com/products/quickspecs/North_America/80018_na.html

Also check out the following web page for a list of manuals that can
be ordered (but evidently are not available online).  Of specific
interest is part number EK-ALXLA-UG, "Alpha  XL User's Guide
(English)".  Kind of sucks that you have to buy it, but I guess it has
not been high priority for DEC/Compaq to put older documents online.
http://www.compaq.com/CAS-Catalog/das04ahm.html

There are several documents for the Celebris XL series, however.  They
were named Celebris XL 5xxx and 6xxx (5 for Pentium, 6 for Pentium
Pro, and xxx for the speed in MHz).  There were even DP (dual
processor) versions.  Perhaps some of those documents will help.  Of
particular interest is the Service Maintenance Manual.  It may be
somewhat useful for the Alpha version since most of the box, beyond
the CPU daughter card) is the same.
http://www4.support.compaq.com/documentation/pcdoc/pdf/sd_cexl.pdf

Good Luck!

Wayne Rigby



