I just did an SRM install on my own Alphastation 200 4/233 (Avanti) from
RH7.0 disks. I did the install process entirely from the CD-ROM drive.
Here are the tricks I found that got around the same kernel panic:
1: Boot from the RH7 CD and do the installation from there.
2: Partition your disk using fdisk and remember this: Your first partition
on the disk should be about 10M unused.  (You're going to need it to install
aboot later.)
3: When the installation is finished, DO NOT REBOOT until you have completed
this task:
    Switch to the shell console (CTRL+ALT+F2 [?] ) and follow (sort of) the
instructions in the how-to
    regarding installation of aboot.  You'll have to do some exploring
though to locate the files and
    binaries though.  I found mine branched off of /mnt/dev/<I forget what
this was>, which is a virtual fs
    that is mounted for the installer.

After that, you should be able to power up to the SRM console and  "boot
<devicename>" straight into Linux, No need for MILO on the AS200 4/233.  For
future boots you will only need to "boot" from the SRM console.

Now my challenge is getting X to work.  I have Digital's ZLXp-E2 24-Plane
8MB video card feeding a Princeton Graphics Ultra 17+.  I see a line at the
end of X -probeonly indicating that Digital TGA only supports one modeline
so I'll play with that next.

Keith.
