>question is:
>
>On my current box I am running 2 IDE quantum ATA-66 drives
>on 2 pci promise controllers in software mirror.
>
>Would I be able to move these to a Alpha box and have it run?  
>Is there something special I would need to do with this?
>
>This box will need the two hda controllers, plus sound, 
>video and 2 ethernets, so if none of this is onboard 
>I would need 6 pci slots.
>
>Any recommendations?
>I am thinking of buying new, but this is for home use,
>so don't get too crazy on cost.

Some systems support ATA-66, and others don't.  I can't help you with that 
question.
Suppose you get a system that does support the ATA.  You can then hook up 
the drives, however, none of the software that is already installed will 
operate.  This is because Alphas use different machine code, so none of the 
binaries will work.  You'll need to obtain Linux installation CDs for 
Alpha.  (Sometimes also called AXP.)
Most machines come with scsi controllers and one ethernet.  Some come with 
sound, some come with video.  You want to make sure that the additional 
hardware you get is supported by Linux.  I suggest browsing through Red 
Hat's supported hardware list (since you say you'll use Red Hat.)  Whenever 
possible, if you buy a DEC card, you can be absolutely guaranteed that 
they've adhered to the standards, made it work on Alpha, and you just need 
to make sure the OS has a driver.  The driver for DEC cards on a Linux 
Alpha system is almost guaranteed.

I've made a faq that is (somewhat) aimed at helping people coming from an 
x86 background, who are new to Alphas.  You can find it at 
http://www.newfieldinn.com/faq


